#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075026 :tutorial/content "The shortest distance between a point and a line is the length of the line segment that connects the point to the line and is perpendicular to the line.\n\nFor example, consider the point ![](images/e2/e2002597cf3d8b01e24e7b4d2c6daf6fc9afb0afb3c6140877abfc3bf1be02c4.svg) and the horizontal line ![](images/06/06444505f504ecd01a91d4846b46bf202caafa9d9f756958483262f69b5db14b.svg) shown below:\n\n![](images/8c/8cba6df956e809981ac4a3ae78a6f6843be0ca8561fbcadd9d9ba98f2a5325cc.png)\n\nTo find the distance between the point and the line, we construct a segment with its endpoints on the point and the line, and which is perpendicular to the line\n\n![](images/80/802b30a788850457206716430755da2d5e3c6b3a53633c95626e49ee668d2507.png)\n\nThe distance between the point and the line is the length of this segment, which we compute as the absolute value of the difference in ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-coordinates of the point and the line:\n\n![](images/11/11e3682c2b811da299d9c475bd0ba3684d0c7f68e5677d925744b37026c47b0c.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "1d8ad60c-5b38-530d-abc1-d03020dca8b2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7819 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075027 :tutorial/content "Consider the line $l$ with the equation $y=-x + 6$ and the point $Q(2,1)$ that does not lie on $l.$\n\n![](images/83/8353825890413432e74d30512219cb95edc76fe8c24e7a824d6893c745c48bd1.png)\n\nSuppose we wish to find the point $P$ that lies on $l$ that's closest to the point $(2,1).$ \n\n![](images/f8/f880cfde6bdfe07c6804609f9b052189632afcde4907207de5245298aea9ea6e.png)\n\nNow, here's the key idea: $P$ is the point that lies on $l$ *and* lies on the line *perpendicular* to $l$ that passes through $Q.$\n\nSo, to find $P,$ we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we find the line perpendicular to $l$ that passes through $Q.$\n\n- Then, we find the intersection of the two lines.\n\nWe know that one endpoint of the segment is [math](2,1).[/math] To find the other endpoint, we find the equation of the line that is perpendicular to [math]y=-x+6[/math] and passes through the point [math](2,1),[/math] and then find where it intersects [math]y=-x+6.[/math]\n\nThe line $y=-x + 6$ has a slope of $-1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $1$ (the negative reciprocal). It also must pass through the point $(2,1).$ So, using point-slope form, we find that the equation of the perpendicular line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =1\\cdot (x-2) \\\\ y-1 & =x-2 \\\\ y & =x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the lines $y=-x+6$ and $y=x-1$ intersect, we set the equations equal to each other and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+6 & =x-1 \\\\ 6+1 & =x+x \\\\ 7 & =2x \\\\ x & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\dfrac{7}{2}$ into either of the two equations gives $y=\\dfrac{5}{2}.$ So, the coordinates of $P$ are $\\left( \\dfrac{7}{2}, \\dfrac{5}{2} \\right).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8fd7f6d4-4f53-50f8-9d72-cceb3d3315ec" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7820 :tutorial/title "Finding the Point on a Line Closest to Another Point"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075028 :tutorial/content "Earlier, we found that the point $P\\left(\\dfrac72,\\dfrac52\\right)$ lies on the line $y=-x+6$ and is the closest point on this line to the point $Q(2,1).$\n\n![](images/11/11901390427d5cd79af514a589175044cafebf47becdae84045005f4564de090.png)\n\nTo calculate the shortest *distance* between $Q$ and the line, we compute the distance between $P$ and $Q$ using the distance formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})}^{2}+{({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{7}{2}-2)}^{2}+{(\\frac{5}{2}-1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{9}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shortest distance between the line $y=-x+6$ and $Q(2,1)$ is $\\dfrac{ 3 }{ \\sqrt{2} } .$" :tutorial/id #uuid "fb4d84ad-bfc1-5e9f-9da6-1447abdc11bc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7916 :tutorial/title "The Shortest Distance Between a Point and a Line"}]}
